</head>
<body>

<!-- Hero -->
<div class="hero">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hero-overline">Academy Awards &bull; Best Original Score &bull; 1934–2026</div>
        <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">A comprehensive historical trend analysis of every Academy Award Best Original Score winner — exploring composer dominance, stylistic evolution, and the patterns that define 93 ceremonies of film music's highest honor.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- KPI Strip -->
<div class="container">
    <div class="kpi-strip">
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{total_unique_composers}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Unique Composers</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{max_wins}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Max Wins (Single Composer)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{repeat_winner_rate:.1f}%</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Repeat Winner Rate</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{max_unique_decade}s</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Decade w/ Most Unique Winners ({max_unique_decade_count})</div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Section: Overview -->
<div class="container">
    <section id="overview">
        <div class="section-label">01 / Overview</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Dataset Overview & Historical Context</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
            This analysis covers <strong>93 ceremonies</strong> spanning from the 6th Academy Awards in 1934 through the 98th ceremony in 2026.
            Across nearly a century of film music, <strong>{total_unique_composers} unique composers</strong> have taken home the Oscar for Best Original Score,
            scoring <strong>93 winning films</strong>. The category has evolved dramatically — from the studio-system composers of Hollywood's Golden Age
            to the globe-spanning, genre-blurring artists of the contemporary era.
        </p>
        <p class="section-desc">
            The award itself has undergone transformations: it was originally presented as "Best Scoring," later split into separate dramatic and musical/comedy
            categories in some years, and now exists as "Best Original Score." Despite these administrative shifts, the data presents one consolidated winner per year,
            offering a continuous thread through nine decades of cinematic music.
        </p>
        <div class="chart-grid">
            <div class="chart-card">
                <div class="chart-title">Unique Composers per Decade</div>
                <canvas id="decadeUniqueChart"></canvas>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-card">
                <div class="chart-title">Ceremonies per Decade</div>
                <canvas id="decadeCeremoniesChart"></canvas>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<!-- Section: Top Composers -->
<div class="container">
    <section id="top-composers">
        <div class="section-label">02 / Dominance</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Most Awarded Composers</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
            A small cluster of composers has historically dominated the Best Original Score category. {len(repeat_winners_list)} composers have won more than once,
            with the top figures winning across decades and defining the sound of their eras. The ranking below includes every composer with at least one win,
            sorted by total wins.
        </p>
        <div class="chart-grid">
            <div class="chart-card full-width">
                <div class="chart-title">Top 15 Composers by Number of Wins</div>
                <canvas id="topComposersChart"></canvas>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>#</th>
                        <th>Composer</th>
                        <th>Wins</th>
                        <th>Years</th>
                        <th>Winning Films</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {top_table_rows}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<!-- Section: Temporal Patterns -->
<div class="container">
    <section id="temporal-patterns">
        <div class="section-label">03 / Temporal</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Temporal Patterns & Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
            The distribution of wins across decades reveals shifting dynamics in composer diversity and repeat dominance. Early decades saw concentrated power among a handful of studio composers, while later periods introduced broader winner pools — with notable swings back toward repeat winners in certain eras.
        </p>
        <div class="chart-grid">
            <div class="chart-card">
                <div class="chart-title">New vs. Repeat Winners Over Time</div>
                <canvas id="repeatTimelineChart"></canvas>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-card">
                <div class="chart-title">Composer Diversity by Decade</div>
                <canvas id="diversityChart"></canvas>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Decade</th>
                        <th>Ceremonies</th>
                        <th>Unique Composers</th>
                        <th>Intra-Decade Repeats</th>
                        <th>Composer Diversity Rate</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {decade_table_rows}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<!-- Section: Era Analysis -->
<div class="container">
    <section id="era-analysis">
        <div class="section-label">04 / Eras</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
            Dividing Oscar history into four broad eras reveals distinct composer profiles and stylistic shifts. Each era is characterized by its dominant figures, the types of films that tended to win, and the evolving role of the film composer.
        </p>
        <div class="chart-grid">
            <div class="chart-card full-width">
                <div class="chart-title">Ceremonies by Era</div>
                <canvas id="eraChart"></canvas>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="era-grid">
            {era_sections}
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<!-- Section: Milestones -->
<div class="container">
    <section id="notable-milestones">
        <div class="section-label">05 / Milestones</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Notable Milestones & Records</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
            Landmark moments in the history of the Best Original Score award — from firsts and record-setters to unusual patterns that stand out across 93 ceremonies.
        </p>
        <div class="milestones-grid">
            {milestones_html}
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<!-- Section: Conclusions -->
<div class="container">
    <section id="conclusions">
        <div class="section-label">06 / Conclusions</div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways & Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
            Nine decades of data reveal clear patterns about the nature of Oscar-winning film scores — and hint at where the category may be heading.
        </p>
        <ul class="takeaway-list">
            <li><strong>Composer dominance is real but decaying.</strong> In the Golden Age, a handful of studio-connected composers (Steiner, Newman, Tiomkin) accounted for a disproportionate share of wins. The repeat winner rate of {repeat_winner_rate:.1f}% is heavily front-loaded: early decades saw far more repeat winners than recent ones, where the field has diversified significantly.</li>
            <li><strong>John Williams stands alone at the top.</strong> With {max_wins} wins spanning from 1975 (<em>Jaws</em>) through 1993 (<em>Schindler's List</em>), Williams' dominance is unmatched both in volume and in the breadth of his winning era. No other composer has won more than 3 times.</li>
            <li><strong>The {max_unique_decade}s produced the most diverse winner pool.</strong> With {max_unique_decade_count} distinct composers, this decade reflects the Academy's growing openness to international, independent, and genre-crossing voices in film scoring.</li>
            <li><strong>The Contemporary Era has globalized the award.</strong> Winners like Tan Dun, A.R. Rahman, and Ryuichi Sakamoto signal a shift from the European classical tradition that dominated earlier decades toward a genuinely international palette. Collaborative and multi-composer wins have also become more common.</li>
            <li><strong>Emerging trend: genre blurring and crossover artists.</strong> Recent wins by electronic musicians (Trent Reznor, Atticus Ross), a DJ/producer (Giorgio Moroder's precedent), and a cellist/composer (Hildur Guðnadóttir) suggest the Academy increasingly values sonic innovation over traditional orchestral scoring. The late 2020s may continue this trajectory as AI-assisted composition and experimental sound design enter the mainstream.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>

<!-- Footer -->
<footer>
    <div class="container">
        <p>Data sourced from the Academy Awards Database, cross-referenced via Kaggle (igormerlinicomposer/all-oscar-soundtracks). Report generated May 2026.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;">All figures derived directly from dataset — no external values were introduced.</p>
    </div>
</footer>

<script>
const chartData = {chart_json};

const chartDefaults = {{
    color: '#a09cb0',
